  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\dfrac1x$. For simplicity, denote $f(f(x))=f_2(x)$ and so on.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Simplify $f_2(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x$, $x\\ne0$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f_2(x)&=f\\left(\\frac1x\\right)\\\\f_2(x)&=\\frac{1}{1/x}\\\\f_2(x)&=x\\end{align*}$$\nThe original function requires $x\\ne0$, so this restriction remains.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Simplify $f_3(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac1x$, $x\\ne0$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f_3(x)&=f(f_2(x))\\\\f_3(x)&=f(x)\\\\f_3(x)&=\\frac1x\\end{align*}$$\nThe domain remains $x\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p16-q10-c-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hypothesise a formula for ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f_n(x)",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", where ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "n",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " is a positive integer.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p16-q10-hint-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hint: Express your answer as a piecewise-defined function.",
                  "marks": [],
                  "colour": "#268cff"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$f_n(x)=\\begin{cases}x,&n\\text{ even},\\\\\\dfrac1x,&n\\text{ odd},\\end{cases}\\qquad x\\ne0.$$",
          "worked": "Each application takes the reciprocal, so the outputs alternate between $\\frac{1}{x}$ and $x$. Two applications return the original nonzero input.\n\nThus, for positive integers $n$,\n$$f_n(x)=\\begin{cases}x,&n\\text{ even},\\\\\\dfrac1x,&n\\text{ odd},\\end{cases}\\qquad x\\ne0.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
